## Why This Is Asked
Assesses interval DP, proper subproblem decomposition, and practical optimization with prefix sums. Builds intuition for product costs over contiguous blocks.

## Key Concepts
- Interval DP on [i, j]
- Prefix sums for O(1) range sums
- Bottom-up filling by increasing length; base case 0 for single element

## Code Example
```javascript
function minMergeCost(stones){
  const n = stones.length;
  const pref = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++) pref[i+1] = pref[i] + stones[i];
  const sum = (i,j) => pref[j+1]-pref[i];
  const dp = Array.from({length:n}, ()=> Array(n).fill(0));
  for(let len=2; len<=n; len++){
    for(let i=0; i+len-1<n; i++){
      const j = i+len-1;
      let best = Infinity;
      for(let k=i; k<j; k++){
        const cost = dp[i][k] + dp[k+1][j] + sum(i,j);
        if(cost < best) best = cost;
      }
      dp[i][j] = best;
    }
  }
  return n>0 ? dp[0][n-1] : 0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for K-ary merging (K > 2)?
- What optimizations apply if n is large and many merges share sums?

## Why This Is Asked
Beginner DP concepts with a new angle: counting unordered combinations (coin change ways) and contrasting memoization with tabulation.

## Key Concepts
- Dynamic programming (top-down memoization)
- Tabulation (bottom-up DP)
- Unordered combinations vs permutations
- Space optimization (1D DP)

## Code Example
```javascript
function countWaysTopDown(coins, target) {
  const n = coins.length;
  const memo = new Map();
  function dfs(i, rem) {
    if (rem === 0) return 1;
    if (rem < 0 || i === n) return 0;
    const key = i + '|' + rem;
    if (memo.has(key)) return memo.get(key);
    const res = dfs(i + 1, rem) + dfs(i, rem - coins[i]);
    memo.set(key, res);
    return res;
  }
  return dfs(0, target);
}

function countWaysBottomUp(coins, target) {
  const dp = new Array(target + 1).fill(0);
  dp[0] = 1;
  for (const c of coins) {
    for (let r = c; r <= target; r++) {
      dp[r] += dp[r - c];
    }
  }
  return dp[target];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you count permutations (order matters) instead of combinations?
- How would you handle very large targets or mod results to avoid overflow in languages with fixed integer sizes?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to model DP with a multi‑dimensional state and non‑trivial transitions, mixing path constraints with exact move counts. It also probes memoization vs bottom‑up, boundary handling, and memory optimizations essential for large grids.

## Key Concepts
- 3D DP state (i, j, t) representing position and moves
- Transitions from (i-1,j,t-1), (i,j-1,t-1), (i-1,j-1,t-1)
- -INF for invalid states; base cases
- Memory optimization via rolling arrays
- Trade-offs between readability (memoized DFS) and performance (tabulation)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxGridSum(grid, k){
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const memo = Array.from({length:n}, ()=> Array.from({length:m}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(NEG)));
  function dfs(i,j,t){
    if(i<0||j<0||t<0) return NEG;
    if(i===0&&j===0&&t===0) return grid[0][0];
    if(memo[i][j][t]!==NEG) return memo[i][j][t];
    const best = Math.max(dfs(i-1,j,t-1), dfs(i,j-1,t-1), dfs(i-1,j-1,t-1));
    memo[i][j][t] = (best===NEG)? NEG : grid[i][j] + best;
    return memo[i][j][t];
  }
  return dfs(n-1,m-1,k);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you reconstruct the path achieving the maximum sum?
- How would this change if some moves were disallowed or weights negative?

