- How to extend to at-most-m or negative values handling?
- How would you optimize to O(m) space with path reconstruction?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests understanding of handling duplicate-heavy inputs efficiently and the ability to implement in-place sorting algorithms with optimized space usage.

## Key Concepts
- **3-way partitioning**: Separates elements into three groups: less than, equal to, and greater than the pivot
- **In-place quicksort**: Achieves O(1) auxiliary space (excluding recursion stack)
- **Pivot selection**: Random sampling avoids worst-case behavior on sorted inputs; median-of-three provides more robust estimates
- **Complexity analysis**: Best/average O(n log n); worst O(n²) when pivot consistently produces imbalanced partitions

## Code Example
```javascript
function threeWayQuicksort(a, lo = 0, hi = a.length - 1) {
  if (lo >= hi) return;
  const pivot = a[Math.floor(Math.random() * (hi - lo + 1)) + lo];
  let lt = lo, i = lo, gt = hi;
  while (i <= gt) {
    if (a[i] < pivot) {
      [a[lt], a[i]] = [a[i], a[lt]];
      lt++; i++;
    } else if (a[i] > pivot) {
      [a[i], a[gt]] = [a[gt], a[i]];
      gt--;
    } else {
      i++;
    }
  }
  threeWayQuicksort(a, lo, lt - 1);
  threeWayQuicksort(a, gt + 1, hi);
}
```

## Complexity Analysis
- **Best case**: O(n log n) — partitions remain balanced
- **Average case**: O(n log n) — expected with random pivot
- **Worst case**: O(n²) — occurs with sorted input and poor pivot (e.g., always picking first or last element)

## Pivot Selection Trade-offs
| Strategy | Pros | Cons |
|----------|------|------|
| Random | Simple, avoids adversarial input | Still possible to pick poor pivots |
| Median-of-three | More robust estimates | Slightly more computation |

The 3-way partition is particularly valuable for arrays with many duplicates, as it reduces unnecessary swaps and comparisons for elements equal to the pivot.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to combine hash maps, deques, and heaps to implement a sliding-window top-K feature in real time.

## Key Concepts
- Sliding window with per-player aggregates
- Lazy deletions in a heap to avoid frequent rebalancing
- Expiration management via per-player deques
- Trade-offs between memory usage and update/query latency

## Code Example
```javascript
class Leaderboard {
  constructor(W) {
    this.W = W;
    this.map = new Map();
    this.heap = [];
  }

  addEvent(pid, delta, ts) {
    const record = this.map.get(pid) || { sum: 0, deque: [] };
    record.deque.push({ ts, delta });
    record.sum += delta;

    while (record.deque.length && record.deque[0].ts < ts - this.W) {
      record.sum -= record.deque.shift().delta;
    }

    this.map.set(pid, record);
    this.heap.push({ sum: record.sum, pid });
  }

  topK(now, K) {
    const results = [];
    for (let i = 0; i < K; i++) {
      while (this.heap.length) {
        const top = this.heap[0];
        const record = this.map.get(top.pid);
        if (!record || record.sum !== top.sum) {
          heapPop(this.heap);
        } else {
          results.push(this.heapPop(this.heap).pid);
          break;
        }
      }
    }
    return results;
  }
}
```

## Data Structure Overview
| Component | Purpose | Complexity |
|-----------|---------|-------------|
| Hash Map | O(1) lookup per player | O(n) memory |
| Per-player Deque | Track event history, expire old entries | O(1) amortized push/shift |
| Max-Heap | Maintain top players efficiently | O(log n) push, O(1) peek |

## Time & Space Complexity
- **addEvent**: O(log n) amortized—each event enters and leaves the deque at most once
- **topK**: O(K log n)—each invalid entry is popped at most once across all queries
- **Memory**: O(n) for the hash map and deques; O(n) worst-case for the heap

## Follow-up Questions
- How to tighten the lazy deletion cost when the heap grows large?
- Can a segment tree or Fenwick tree provide stricter time guarantees?
- How would you handle concurrent updates from multiple streams?

